<link rel="stylesheet" href="/s-13vep8.css"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://nclexsim.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Nursing simulation scenarios","item":"https://nclexsim.com/nursing-simulation-scenarios"}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"SoftwareApplication","name":"Vexcura nursing simulation drills","description":"Virtual, screen-based nursing simulation: an adaptive, physiology-driven deteriorating-patient drill in an academic EHR, Telemetry and EKG, Assessment and Auscultation, ACLS megacodes, bedside cases and NCLEX topic drills.","url":"https://nclexsim.com/nursing-simulation-scenarios","applicationCategory":"EducationalApplication","applicationSubCategory":"Nursing education","operatingSystem":"Any (web browser)","featureList":["Adaptive deteriorating-patient drill with 59 hidden cases in 4 tiers","Physiology-driven patient course that responds to nursing care","SBAR call to a simulated provider by phone or Secure Chat","Debrief scored by competency","Telemetry and EKG simulation with calipers and 53 rhythms","Central station alarm practice","Assessment and auscultation simulation with 32 synthesized sounds","Seven ACLS megacode scenarios","Bedside cases that change in real time","10-question topic drills in all 8 NCLEX client needs categories","Instructor assignment with due dates"],"audience":{"@type":"EducationalAudience","educationalRole":"teacher","audienceType":"Nursing schools, nursing faculty and clinical instructors"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://nclexsim.com/#org"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"HowTo","name":"How to run a deteriorating-patient drill in Vexcura","step":[{"@type":"HowToStep","position":1,"text":"Start a drill: Open a practice patient in the academic EHR and choose Start a drill. Pick Auto difficulty (recommended) or an unlocked tier, and a pace: slow, standard or fast. The drill uses the open chart when a case fits it; otherwise a new admission opens."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":2,"text":"Take report and watch for cues: Handoff, call lights, the nursing assistant, monitor alarms, family members and lab calls bring cues in as they happen, while other patients' requests compete for attention. The problem is never named."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":3,"text":"Assess: Take vital signs, do focused assessments, listen with the stethoscope and check results. Findings file to the flowsheet at the drill's chart time."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":4,"text":"Act: Use nursing actions such as oxygen, positioning, suction, direct pressure or holding a medication, within the standing protocols on the chart. Harmful actions are caught when they happen."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":5,"text":"Call the provider with SBAR: Call by phone or page in Secure Chat. The simulated provider asks for anything missing and gives orders based on what was reported; read them back and give them from the MAR. Call a rapid response or a code when the patient meets the criteria."},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":6,"text":"Read the debrief: The debrief shows the patient's course with every action marked, the score for each competency, missed and harmful actions with their sources, teaching points and debrief questions. It's saved to the chart's notes, so it shows up in handoff, the PDF chart report and the instructor's view."}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the deteriorating-patient drill?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A screen-based simulation inside the academic EHR. A practice patient's condition changes over time from a physiology model that responds to what the student does. The case stays hidden until the debrief, so the student has to recognize the change, act, call the provider with SBAR and check that it worked."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many cases are there?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"59, in 4 tiers: 10 single-problem med-surg cases, 23 with complications or atypical presentations, 9 with competing priorities, and 17 in the ICU and emergency department. Each run is a fresh course: onset speed, severity, presentation, comorbidities and distractors vary."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does adaptive difficulty work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"With Auto difficulty, each drill updates the student's rating. Students move up a tier as they improve and step back one tier after two tough runs. Instructors can choose a specific case for an assignment, and the student still doesn't see its name until the debrief."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How are drills scored?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By competency: recognize cues 20 points, prioritize 15, take action 25, escalate and SBAR 20, document 10 and evaluate response 10. The debrief shows each missed or harmful action with its source, the time to recognition and the time to escalation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do drills relate to the NCLEX?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Next Generation NCLEX is built on the NCSBN Clinical Judgment Measurement Model. The drill practices the same thinking on one patient under time pressure. A drill score is practice feedback, not a prediction of an NCLEX result. Vexcura isn't affiliated with NCSBN."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What's in Telemetry and EKG?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"53 computer-generated rhythms with calipers for R-R, PR, QRS and QT, rate methods and QTc, an adaptive challenge mode, a central station where students acknowledge alarms, and Code / ACLS scenarios. Strips are generated from a beat model, not recordings."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What's in Assessment and Auscultation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"32 synthesized heart, lung, bowel and vascular sounds on a body map, with diaphragm and bell, breathing and position controls, practice clients, a full head-to-toe assessment charted within defined limits or as exceptions, and a challenge mode. There are no recordings; headphones help with low sounds."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is AI used in drills?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The orders and scoring are rule-based, so a drill works the same if AI is unavailable. Optional AI wording rewords the provider's replies and writes part of the debrief, and it's labeled that way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can instructors assign drills?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. In the instructor workspace's Assign tab, pick the students, then a topic drill by category, a megacode or a bedside case, and a due date. For the deteriorating-patient drill, instructors can choose a case and copy an assignment link. Debriefs are saved to the chart for the instructor's view."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do drills replace our simulation lab?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. They're screen-based practice that students can repeat on their own, and they don't replace your program's simulation curriculum or your school's protocols."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is real patient information used?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Every patient is made up. The practice EHR uses hospital days instead of dates, has no fields for names or record numbers, and warns students who type something that looks like an identifier."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does it cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Drills are included in every plan, and students and instructors never pay. The school or facility licenses Vexcura: Per student $199 per student seat; Site 100 $15,000; Site 250 $30,000; Site 500 $50,000; Custom Custom quote, per year."}}]}</script></head><body><header class="top"><div class="w"><a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Vexcura home"><svg class="vxw" viewBox="-2 -86 472 96" aria-hidden="true"><style>@property --vxa{syntax:"<color>";inherits:true;initial-value:#4b4f86}@property --vxb{syntax:"<color>";inherits:true;initial-value:#d77a6a}@property --vxc{syntax:"<color>";inherits:true;initial-value:#f8c27a}@property --vxrb{syntax:"<color>";inherits:true;initial-value:#f8c27a}@property --vxrc{syntax:"<color>";inherits:true;initial-value:#d77a6a}:root{--vxa:#4b4f86;--vxb:#d77a6a;--vxc:#f8c27a;--vxrb:#f8c27a;--vxrc:#d77a6a;transition:--vxa 4s ease,--vxb 4s ease,--vxc 4s ease,--vxrb 4s ease,--vxrc 4s ease}html[data-vxscene="dawn"]{--vxa:#7e6a8e;--vxb:#d9826f;--vxc:#eaa874;--vxrb:#fbd3c0;--vxrc:#f2a98e}html[data-vxscene="morning"]{--vxa:#1d5670;--vxb:#3f8fae;--vxc:#e2a648;--vxrb:#bfe3f1;--vxrc:#ffe2a0}html[data-vxscene="midday"]{--vxa:#1d5670;--vxb:#2f85a8;--vxc:#56acd3;--vxrb:#a9dcf1;--vxrc:#6fbfe6}html[data-vxscene="afternoon"]{--vxa:#356f8f;--vxb:#c99a3e;--vxc:#b8642c;--vxrb:#f6e2bc;--vxrc:#e8b45a}html[data-vxscene="sunset"]{--vxa:#4b4f86;--vxb:#d77a6a;--vxc:#f8c27a;--vxrb:#f8c27a;--vxrc:#d77a6a}html[data-vxscene="night"]{--vxa:#1d3557;--vxb:#4b6a9a;--vxc:#d9b25a;--vxrb:#c9d6ea;--vxrc:#ffd77a}@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){:root{transition:none}}</style><script>(function(){var h=new Date().getHours(),k=h<5?"night":h<8?"dawn":h<11?"morning":h<14?"midday":h<17?"afternoon":h<20?"sunset":"night";document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-vxscene",k)})()</script><defs><linearGradient id="sxrW" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="0" y1="0" x2="227.0" y2="0"><stop offset="0" style="stop-color:var(--vxa);stop-opacity:1"/><stop offset=".5" style="stop-color:var(--vxb);stop-opacity:1"/><stop offset="1" style="stop-color:var(--vxc);stop-opacity:1"/></linearGradient><linearGradient id="sxrWr" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="0" y1="0" x2="227.0" y2="0"><stop offset="0" style="stop-color:#ffffff;stop-opacity:1"/><stop offset=".5" style="stop-color:var(--vxrb);stop-opacity:1"/><stop offset="1" style="stop-color:var(--vxrc);stop-opacity:1"/></linearGradient><linearGradient id="sxrB" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="16" y1="-72" x2="27" y2="4"><stop offset="0" style="stop-color:var(--vxb);stop-opacity:1"/><stop offset="1" style="stop-color:var(--vxc);stop-opacity:1"/></linearGradient><linearGradient id="sxrBr" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="16" y1="-72" x2="27" y2="4"><stop offset="0" style="stop-color:#ffffff;stop-opacity:1"/><stop offset="1" style="stop-color:var(--vxrc);stop-opacity:1"/></linearGradient><linearGradient id="sxrF" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x1="0" y1="4" x2="25" y2="-72"><stop offset="0" style="stop-color:var(--vxc);stop-opacity:0"/><stop offset=".55" style="stop-color:var(--vxc);stop-opacity:0.55"/><stop offset="1" style="stop-color:var(--vxc);stop-opacity:0.95"/></linearGradient></defs><path class="bmf" d="M14 -72h11L11 4H0z" fill="url(#sxrF)"/><path class="bmb" d="M30 -72h11L27 4H16z" fill="url(#sxrBr)"/><g transform="translate(50 0)"><path d="M47.1 0L21.5 0L9.3-68.8L32.1-68.8L36-37Q36.2-35.7 36.5-33.2Q36.8-30.7 37.1-27.9Q37.5-25.1 37.8-22.8L37.8-22.8L38.2-22.8Q39.4-25.2 40.5-27.6Q41.6-29.9 42.8-32.3Q43.9-34.7 45-37L45-37L60.2-68.8L83.6-68.8L47.1 0ZM97.1 1.2L97.1 1.2Q88.5 1.2 82.3-1.2Q76.1-3.5 72.8-8.3Q69.4-13.2 69.4-20.7L69.4-20.7Q69.4-22.9 69.5-25.0Q69.7-27 70.1-28.9L70.1-28.9Q71.6-37.3 76.1-42.9Q80.6-48.4 87.5-51.2Q94.3-54 102.6-54L102.6-54Q111.3-54 117.3-51.8Q123.4-49.6 126.5-45.1Q129.7-40.5 129.7-33.4L129.7-33.4Q129.7-31.8 129.5-29.6Q129.3-27.4 128.5-23L128.5-23L88.9-23L88.7-21.3Q88.5-20.2 88.5-19.4L88.5-19.4Q88.5-16.9 89.4-15.1Q90.3-13.3 92.3-12.4Q94.3-11.5 97.4-11.5L97.4-11.5Q100.1-11.5 102.0-12.1Q104-12.7 105.3-13.8Q106.6-14.8 107.4-16.2Q108.2-17.6 108.5-19.2L108.5-19.2L127.8-19.2Q127-14.5 124.7-10.7Q122.3-6.9 118.5-4.3Q114.6-1.6 109.3-0.2Q103.9 1.2 97.1 1.2ZM90.6-32.4L90.6-32.4L110-32.4Q110.1-32.9 110.2-33.5Q110.2-34.1 110.2-34.5L110.2-34.5Q110.2-36.7 10