    <p class="mh">Guides</p><a class="mt" href="/nclex-study-guides"><b>Free study guides</b><small>Printable PDFs</small></a><a class="mt" href="/nclex-at-home"><b>NCLEX at home</b><small>What's coming</small></a><a class="mt" href="/nclex-prep-comparison"><b>Compare NCLEX prep</b><small>What each one includes</small></a><a class="mt" href="/about"><b>About Vexcura</b><small>Who built it</small></a>
    <p class="mh">Work with us</p><a class="mt" href="/careers"><b>Careers</b><small>Clinical education sales for RNs and LPNs</small></a>
    <p class="mh">Account</p><a class="mt" href="/#signup"><b>Join with a school code</b><small>Or request access</small></a><a class="mt" href="/#signin"><b>Sign in</b><small>Already have one</small></a>
   </div></details></nav></div></header><script>(function(){var d=document.querySelector(".mnav");if(!d)return;document.addEventListener("click",function(e){if(d.open&&!d.contains(e.target))d.open=false});document.addEventListener("keydown",function(e){if(e.key==="Escape"&&d.open){d.open=false;d.querySelector("summary").focus()}})})();</script><main><nav class="crumb w" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <span>Nursing simulation scenarios</span></nav><section class="hero w hv"><div><h1>Nursing Simulation Scenarios for Clinical Judgment Practice</h1><p class="lead">Virtual clinical simulation for nursing students. In the deteriorating-patient drill, a practice patient's physiology responds to what the student does, and the problem stays hidden. The student has to notice the change, act, call the provider with SBAR and check that it worked, then read a debrief scored by competency.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/for-nursing-schools#pilot">Request a free semester pilot</a><a class="btn alt" href="tel:+17626772802">Call 762-677-2802</a></div></div><figure class="shot duo"><div class="duo-s"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/dx-run-1000.webp" srcset="/img/dx-run-1440.webp 1440w, /img/dx-run-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 860px) calc(100vw - 72px), 560px" width="1000" height="625" alt="A deteriorating-patient drill starting on a made-up practice patient: the drill panel with a call light, handoff, a respiratory assessment filed to flowsheets, and Assess, Act and Call actions" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div></div><div class="dv dv-p dv-po" style="--sb:#1a4e71"><div class="dv-s"><div class="dv-sb lt" aria-hidden="true"><time class="vx-now" data-f="sb"></time><span class="dv-isl"></span><span class="dv-ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 18 12" width="17" height="11"><rect x="0" y="8" width="3" height="4" rx=".8"/><rect x="5" y="5.5" width="3" height="6.5" rx=".8"/><rect x="10" y="3" width="3" height="9" rx=".8"/><rect x="15" y="0" width="3" height="12" rx=".8"/></svg><svg viewBox="0 0 16 12" width="15" height="11"><path d="M8 11.6 5.6 9.2a3.4 3.4 0 0 1 4.8 0z"/><path d="M3.4 7A6.5 6.5 0 0 1 12.6 7l-1.3 1.3a4.7 4.7 0 0 0-6.6 0z"/><path d="M1.1 4.7a9.7 9.7 0 0 1 13.8 0l-1.3 1.3a7.9 7.9 0 0 0-11.2 0z"/></svg><svg viewBox="0 0 27 13" width="25" height="12"><rect x=".6" y=".6" width="22.8" height="11.8" rx="3.4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity=".4" stroke-width="1.2"/><rect x="2.4" y="2.4" width="17.6" height="8.2" rx="2"/><path d="M25 4.4v4.2c.9-.3 1.4-1.1 1.4-2.1s-.5-1.8-1.4-2.1z" fill-opacity=".45"/></svg></span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/dx-phone-780.webp" srcset="/img/dx-phone-400.webp 400w, /img/dx-phone-780.webp 780w" sizes="(max-width: 860px) 30vw, 160px" width="780" height="1600" alt="A drill on a phone: the nursing assistant reports new slurred speech and facial droop on a made-up patient, with Assess, Act and Call tabs" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div></div></div><figcaption>A deteriorating-patient drill on made-up practice patients, on a laptop and a phone.</figcaption></figure></section><div class="w"><h2>What's included</h2><p>Short, repeatable clinical simulations on a screen. Students practice the moment a patient starts to go wrong: what they notice, what they do first, who they call and whether it worked.</p><div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Simulation</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Deteriorating-patient drill</b></td><td>59 hidden cases in 4 tiers, from single-problem med-surg to the ICU and emergency department. Physiology-driven and adaptive.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Telemetry and EKG</b></td><td>53 computer-generated rhythms: Learn, Practice with calipers, Challenge, a central station with alarms, and Code / ACLS.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Assessment and Auscultation</b></td><td>32 synthesized heart, lung, bowel and vascular sounds on a body map, practice clients and a full head-to-toe assessment.</td></tr><tr><td><b>ACLS megacodes</b></td><td>Seven codes on a live monitor: VF arrest on telemetry, symptomatic bradycardia, unstable tachycardia, PEA from post-op bleeding, a massive PE or septic shock, and asystole from an opioid overdose.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bedside cases</b></td><td>Three patients who change in real time at the bedside: shortness of breath after hip surgery, acting &quot;weird&quot; after skipping lunch, and &quot;just not herself&quot;.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Topic drills</b></td><td>10 NCLEX-style questions in any of the 8 client needs categories, with the rationale after each one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="deteriorating-patient">The deteriorating-patient drill</h2><p>Each drill runs inside the academic EHR on a practice patient. Vital signs, labs, rhythm, exam findings and imaging come from a physiology model, so the patient gets worse without the right care and improves with it: oxygen, positioning, fluids, medications, drips and escalation all change the course. The case is hidden until the debrief, and each run is different: onset speed, severity, presentation, comorbidities and distractors vary.</p>
       <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Level</th><th>Cases</th><th>What students face</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Tier 1</b></td><td>Single-problem med-surg</td><td>10</td><td>One problem on a med-surg patient, such as hypoglycemia, opioid oversedation or fluid volume deficit.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tier 2</b></td><td>Complications and atypical presentations</td><td>23</td><td>Complications and atypical presentations, such as a pulmonary embolism after surgery, an atypical NSTEMI, DKA, a transfusion reaction or compartment syndrome.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tier 3</b></td><td>Multiple problems, competing priorities</td><td>9</td><td>More than one problem at once, such as sepsis in a patient with heart failure, or four patients and one nurse.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tier 4</b></td><td>ICU and emergency department</td><td>17</td><td>Monitored patients such as septic shock on vasopressors, STEMI with cardiogenic shock, a tension pneumothorax on the ventilator or ED triage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="split"><div class="st"><h2>Adaptive difficulty</h2><p>With Auto difficulty, every drill updates the student's rating. Students move up a tier as they improve and step back a tier after two tough runs, and the next case targets their weakest competency. The pace sets how fast simulated time runs: slow, standard or fast.</p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/dx-start-1000.webp" srcset="/img/dx-start-1600.webp 1600w, /img/dx-start-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="Starting a drill: the student's tier and progress toward the next one, Auto difficulty and the pace. The problem stays hidden until the debrief" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>Starting a drill: tier, progress, difficulty and pace. Fictional patient.</figcaption></figure></div><h2>How a drill runs</h2><ol><li><b>Start a drill.</b> Open a practice patient in the academic EHR and choose Start a drill. Pick Auto difficulty (recommended) or an unlocked tier, and a pace: slow, standard or fast. The drill uses the open chart when a case fits it; otherwise a new admission opens.</li><li><b>Take report and watch for cues.</b> Handoff, call lights, the nursing assistant, monitor alarms, family members and lab calls bring cues in as they happen, while other patients' requests compete for attention. The problem is never named.</li><li><b>Assess.</b> Take vital signs, do focused assessments, listen with the stethoscope and check results. Findings file to the flowsheet at the drill's chart time.</li><li><b>Act.</b> Use nursing actions such as oxygen, positioning, suction, direct pressure or holding a medication, within the standing protocols on the chart. Harmful actions are caught when they happen.</li><li><b>Call the provider with SBAR.</b> Call by phone or page in Secure Chat. The simulated provider asks for anything missing and gives orders based on what was reported; read them back and give them from the MAR. Call a rapid response or a code when the patient meets the criteria.</li><li><b>Read the debrief.</b> The debrief shows the patient's course with every action marked, the score for each competency, missed and harmful actions with their sources, teaching points and debrief questions. It's saved to the chart's notes, so it shows up in handoff, the PDF chart report and the instructor's view.</li></ol><div class="split rev"><div class="st"><h2>SBAR to a simulated provider</h2><p>Students call the way they would on the unit, by phone or by paging in Secure Chat. The simulated provider listens for the data that matters in that case, asks a clarifying question when something is missing, and gives orders based on what was reported. Students read the orders back, accept them into the chart and give medications from the MAR. A rapid response team arrives when called, and takes a handoff.</p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/dx-icu-1000.webp" srcset="/img/dx-icu-1600.webp 1600w, /img/dx-icu-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="A Tier 4 ICU drill on a made-up patient: arterial line and rhythm on the monitor, an alarm, a vasopressor drip to titrate, and the flowsheet beside the drill panel" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>An ICU-tier drill on a made-up patient: monitor, alarm, a drip to titrate and the flowsheet.</figcaption></figure></div><h2 id="scoring">Debrief and scoring by competency</h2><p>The debrief charts the patient's heart rate, MAP and SpO2 over the drill with every cue and action marked, then scores six competencies. Each missed or harmful action is listed with the reason and its source.</p>
       <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Competency</th><th>Points</th><th>What the drill checks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Recognize cues</b></td><td>20</td><td>A focused assessment soon after the first cue; full credit within 5 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Prioritize</b></td><td>15</td><td>The first move fits the problem, decisions in the feed are answered well, and actions come in the right order.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Take action</b></td><td>25</td><td>The required nursing actions and orders, on time, without harmful ones.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Escalate / SBAR</b></td><td>20</td><td>An SBAR call with the data the provider needs, and escalation within 5 minutes once criteria are met.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Document</b></td><td>10</td><td>Assessments and actions charted.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Evaluate response</b></td><td>10</td><td>A reassessment after each intervention, within its window.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
       <figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/dx-debrief-1000.webp" srcset="/img/dx-debrief-1600.webp 1600w, /img/dx-debrief-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="A drill debrief: heart rate, MAP and SpO2 over the drill with the student's actions marked, and the score by competency with each missed action and its source" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>A drill debrief: the patient course and the score by competency. Demo student.</figcaption></figure>
       <p>The orders and scoring are rule-based, so a drill is scored the same way if AI is unavailable. Optional AI wording rewords the provider's replies and writes part of the debrief, labeled that way. Cases cite sources such as the Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021 guidelines, the AHA 2025 adult advanced life support guidelines, the ADA Standards of Care, KDIGO, GOLD and the ASPMN guideline on opioid-induced sedation.</p><h2 id="cjmm">Built for clinical judgment</h2><p>The Next Generation NCLEX measures clinical judgment with the <a href="https://www.ncsbn.org/14798.htm" rel="noopener">NCSBN Clinical Judgment Measurement Model</a>: recognize cues, analyze cues, prioritize hypotheses, generate solutions, take action and evaluate outcomes. The drill practices that thinking on one patient, under time pressure, the way an <a href="/next-generation-nclex">NGN unfolding case study</a> walks through it. A drill score is practice feedback, not a prediction of an NCLEX result, and Vexcura isn't affiliated with NCSBN.</p><h2 id="coordination">From one patient to a full assignment</h2><p>These drills build the judgment a nurse brings to <em>one</em> changing patient. The RN's actual job is coordinating several patients and a team at once: prioritizing across rooms, delegating within scope and handing off safely, the NCLEX-RN's Management of Care category. Vexcura builds up to that with a multi-patient <a href="/nursing-care-coordination#the-shift">Shift simulation</a>. See the whole ladder on <a href="/nursing-care-coordination">nursing care coordination</a>.</p><div class="split"><div class="st"><h2 id="telemetry">Telemetry and EKG</h2><p>53 computer-generated rhythms, from sinus rhythms to AV blocks, paced rhythms and arrest rhythms. Students measure with calipers (R-R, PR, QRS and QT, with snap to the small box), march out P waves, calculate rate and QTc, and name the rhythm. Challenge mode favors the rhythms and steps a student misses. Strips come from a beat model, not recordings.</p><p>At the central station, students watch a unit of monitored patients and acknowledge alarms, including misleading monitor labels. Code / ACLS runs arrest scenarios from the strip.</p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/tele-calipers-1000.webp" srcset="/img/tele-calipers-1600.webp 1600w, /img/tele-calipers-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="Telemetry and EKG practice: calipers measuring a 0.22-second PR interval on a computer-generated lead II and V1 strip, in seconds, small boxes and large boxes" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>Calipers measuring a PR interval on a computer-generated strip.</figcaption></figure></div>
       <figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/tele-station-1000.webp" srcset="/img/tele-station-1600.webp 1600w, /img/tele-station-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="The central station on a fictional telemetry unit: six monitored patients with alarms to acknowledge, including VF, V-tach, leads off and sinus bradycardia" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>The central station on a fictional telemetry unit.</figcaption></figure><div class="split rev"><div class="st"><h2 id="auscultation">Assessment and Auscultation</h2><p>32 synthesized heart, lung, bowel and vascular sounds. Students move the chest piece over a body map from the front, back, sides and neck, switch between diaphragm and bell, ask the client to breathe deeply, hold their breath or cough, and change position, with a sound trace synced to the ECG.</p><p>Practice clients ask students to find the abnormal sounds and where they're heard. The full assessment has them chart head to toe, within defined limits or as exceptions, on a flowsheet. There are no recordings, and headphones help with low-pitched sounds.</p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/ausc-lungs-1000.webp" srcset="/img/ausc-lungs-1600.webp 1600w, /img/ausc-lungs-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="Assessment and Auscultation: the posterior chest with numbered listening points, chest piece, breathing and position controls, and a synthesized sound trace synced to the ECG" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>Listening over the posterior chest.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="split"><div class="st"><h2>ACLS megacodes</h2><p>Seven codes on a live monitor, with defibrillation, pacing, CPR quality and medication timing, then a scored debrief and a resuscitation record to download as a PDF. For practice only; it isn't an American Heart Association course or ACLS certification. <a href="/acls-megacode-simulator">More on the megacode simulator</a></p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/drills-megacode-1000.webp" srcset="/img/drills-megacode-1600.webp 1600w, /img/drills-megacode-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="The ACLS megacode simulator during a VF arrest: live monitor, defibrillator charged to 200 joules and the chest compressions panel" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>A VF arrest megacode. Practice only.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="split rev"><div class="st"><h2>Bedside cases</h2><p>The patient keeps changing while the student works: assess a body system, chart it as within defined limits or as an exception, then call the provider or a rapid response. A case can turn into a code. Bedside cases show the outcome, the time to first escalation, the cues found and whether the student's read of the problem was right. <a href="/nclex-clinical-simulation">More on bedside simulation</a></p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/drills-bedside-1000.webp" srcset="/img/drills-bedside-1600.webp 1600w, /img/drills-bedside-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="A bedside rounds case: a made-up patient after hip surgery with a live monitor, and his assessment findings in the chart beside him" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>A bedside case with assessment findings charted. Made-up patient.</figcaption></figure></div><h2>Connected to the rest of the EHR</h2><ul><li>Bedside assessments in a drill file to the spreadsheet-style flowsheets.</li><li>Monitored patients show their current rhythm strip from Telemetry and EKG.</li><li>"Listen with the stethoscope" opens the matching heart, lung or bowel sound.</li><li>Secure Chat can page the provider during a drill; read-back orders land on the chart, and a rapid response called from chat counts as escalation.</li></ul>
       <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:14.5px">All patients are fictional. The app labels the deteriorating-patient drill, Telemetry and EKG, and Assessment and Auscultation content as AI-assisted, fact-checked and reviewed by a registered nurse (November 2026). Confirm thresholds and protocols against your facility's policies.</p><div class="split rev"><div class="st"><h2 id="assign">How instructors assign drills</h2><ol><li><b>Who:</b> pick students in the instructor workspace's Assign tab.</li><li><b>What:</b> a topic drill by category, or a megacode or bedside case under More practice. For the deteriorating-patient drill, choose a case and copy an assignment link.</li><li><b>When:</b> a due date, once or repeating daily for 3 or 5 days or on weekdays, with an optional note.</li></ol><p>The work lands on each student's worklist in My Chart. Debriefs are saved to the chart's notes and show up in the instructor's view of patients shared with a live clinical group. <a href="/clinical-instructor-tools">Clinical instructor tools</a></p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/drills-topics-1000.webp" srcset="/img/drills-topics-1600.webp 1600w, /img/drills-topics-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="The topic drill picker: a 10-question drill for each of the 8 NCLEX client needs categories" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>The topic drill picker: one drill for each NCLEX client needs category.</figcaption></figure></div><h2 id="coming-soon">Coming soon: virtual clinical days and VR</h2><p class="soon"><b>Coming soon</b> Not available yet</p><p>Vexcura is designing faculty-led, screen-based virtual clinical days built around Tanner's Clinical Judgment Model, with a prebrief, an unfolding case, a structured debrief and faculty sign-off, and is exploring immersive VR simulation. Neither is available yet. <a href="/virtual-nursing-simulation">Virtual nursing simulation: what's live and what's coming</a></p><h2 id="pricing">Included in every plan</h2><p>The deteriorating-patient drill, Telemetry and EKG, Assessment and Auscultation, megacodes, bedside cases and topic drills are included in every Vexcura plan, and students and instructors never pay. Plans differ only in how many students they cover, per year: Per student $199 per student seat · Site 100 $15,000 · Site 250 $30,000 · Site 500 $50,000 · Custom: Custom quote. <a href="/for-nursing-schools#pricing">Pricing for schools</a></p><h2>Common questions</h2><details class="faq"><summary>What is the deteriorating-patient drill?</summary><p>A screen-based simulation inside the academic EHR. A practice patient's condition changes over time from a physiology model that responds to what the student does. The case stays hidden until the debrief, so the student has to recognize the change, act, call the provider with SBAR and check that it worked.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>How many cases are there?</summary><p>59, in 4 tiers: 10 single-problem med-surg cases, 23 with complications or atypical presentations, 9 with competing priorities, and 17 in the ICU and emergency department. Each run is a fresh course: onset speed, severity, presentation, comorbidities and distractors vary.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>How does adaptive difficulty work?</summary><p>With Auto difficulty, each drill updates the student's rating. Students move up a tier as they improve and step back one tier after two tough runs. Instructors can choose a specific case for an assignment, and the student still doesn't see its name until the debrief.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>How are drills scored?</summary><p>By competency: recognize cues 20 points, prioritize 15, take action 25, escalate and SBAR 20, document 10 and evaluate response 10. The debrief shows each missed or harmful action with its source, the time to recognition and the time to escalation.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>How do drills relate to the NCLEX?</summary><p>The Next Generation NCLEX is built on the NCSBN Clinical Judgment Measurement Model. The drill practices the same thinking on one patient under time pressure. A drill score is practice feedback, not a prediction of an NCLEX result. Vexcura isn't affiliated with NCSBN.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>What's in Telemetry and EKG?</summary><p>53 computer-generated rhythms with calipers for R-R, PR, QRS and QT, rate methods and QTc, an adaptive challenge mode, a central station where students acknowledge alarms, and Code / ACLS scenarios. Strips are generated from a beat model, not recordings.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>What's in Assessment and Auscultation?</summary><p>32 synthesized heart, lung, bowel and vascular sounds on a body map, with diaphragm and bell, breathing and position controls, practice clients, a full head-to-toe assessment charted within defined limits or as exceptions, and a challenge mode. There are no recordings; headphones help with low sounds.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>Is AI used in drills?</summary><p>The orders and scoring are rule-based, so a drill works the same if AI is unavailable. Optional AI wording rewords the provider's replies and writes part of the debrief, and it's labeled that way.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>Can instructors assign drills?</summary><p>Yes. In the instructor workspace's Assign tab, pick the students, then a topic drill by category, a megacode or a bedside case, and a due date. For the deteriorating-patient drill, instructors can choose a case and copy an assignment link. Debriefs are saved to the chart for the instructor's view.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>Do drills replace our simulation lab?</summary><p>No. They're screen-based practice that students can repeat on their own, and they don't replace your program's simulation curriculum or your school's protocols.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>Is real patient information used?</summary><p>No. Every patient is made up. The practice EHR uses hospital days instead of dates, has no fields for names or record numbers, and warns students who type something that looks like an identifier.</p></details><details class="faq"><summary>What does it cost?</summary><p>Drills are included in every plan, and students and instructors never pay. The school or facility licenses Vexcura: Per student $199 per student seat; Site 100 $15,000; Site 250 $30,000; Site 500 $50,000; Custom Custom quote, per year.</p></details><h2>More for nursing programs</h2><ul class="rel"><li><a href="/for-nursing-schools">Vexcura for nursing schools</a></li><li><a href="/nursing-care-coordination">Nursing care coordination (Management of Care)</a></li><li><a href="/clinical-instructor-tools">Clinical instructor tools</a></li><li><a href="/academic-ehr-for-nursing-students">Academic EHR for nursing students</a></li><li><a href="/nursing-board-compliance">Board of nursing compliance (GA, TN, AL)</a></li><li><a href="/virtual-nursing-simulation">Virtual nursing simulation (VR coming soon)</a></li><li><a href="/nursing-lms-integration">Canvas, Blackboard, D2L and Moodle integration</a></li></ul><h2>Related</h2><ul class="rel"><li><a href="/nursing-care-coordination">Nursing care coordination (Management of Care)</a></li><li><a href="/virtual-nursing-simulation">Virtual nursing simulation (VR coming soon)</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-clinical-simulation">Bedside clinical simulation</a></li><li><a href="/acls-megacode-simulator">ACLS megacode simulator</a></li><li><a href="/next-generation-nclex">Next Generation NCLEX (NGN) guide</a></li></ul></div><section class="band vxs-band"><div class="vxs-bg" data-vxs-i="0" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="vxs-sh" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="w"><h2>Try Vexcura with one cohort for a semester, free</h2><p>Pick one clinical group or course. Faculty accounts are always free. At the end of the semester you decide whether to license it for your program; there's no obligation to buy, and every plan includes everything. Founding programs: 50% off the first paid year.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/for-nursing-schools#pilot">Request a pilot</a><a class="btn alt" href="/for-nursing-schools">How licensing works</a></div></div></section></main><footer class="foot"><div class="w"><div class="cols">
